        "id": "R1",
        "title": "Power from the Moon: The Tide Mill",
        "questionRange": "1-13",
        "paragraphs": [
          {
            "label": "A",
            "text": "Long before steam engines or electricity, communities on sheltered coasts discovered that the sea itself could turn a millstone. A tide mill works on a simple principle. As the tide rises, water pushes open a gate and fills a large storage pond. When the sea outside begins to fall, the gate swings shut and traps the water inside. Once the level in the estuary has dropped far enough, the miller opens a channel called a sluice, and the stored water pours across a wheel, which drives the grinding machinery. Windmills depended on breezes, and river mills suffered during droughts and frosts, but tide mills followed the predictable rhythm of the moon."
          },
          {
            "label": "B",
            "text": "That predictability came at a price. A full pond could keep the wheel turning for only about three hours, so a mill could work two short shifts a day. Because high tide arrives roughly fifty minutes later each day, the miller's working hours drifted constantly: one week he might be grinding at midnight, the next at dawn. Millers' families learned to organise meals and sleep around a tide table rather than a clock, and apprentices were often hired as much for their willingness to work at odd hours as for their skill."
          },
          {
            "label": "C",
            "text": "Building a tide mill required considerable capital. The pond had to be enclosed by a thick wall of earth and stone, and the gates had to hold back enormous volumes of water without leaking. Saltwick Mill, built on a muddy creek in 1742, had a pond covering about eleven hectares, and it took four years to complete. Its three investors, all grain merchants, hoped to recover their outlay within a decade. However, silt carried in by every tide clogged the creek, and the channel had to be dredged each spring. It was not until 1768 that the mill first made a profit."
          },
          {
            "label": "D",
            "text": "Despite such costs, tide mills multiplied. A survey in 1850 recorded about 140 of them along a single stretch of coastline, many supplying flour to expanding port towns. Bakers valued the steady output, while ship captains appreciated that sacks could be loaded directly from the mill's own quay. Some owners diversified. Saltwick began pressing oilseed in 1790 and later added a second wheel to saw timber, which gave the mill income in months when grain prices were low."
          },
          {
            "label": "E",
            "text": "Decline arrived with the steam engine, which could be installed anywhere and was not tied to the tide. Coal-fired roller mills built beside railway stations could operate around the clock, and by the 1880s imported wheat was cheaper to process in large port mills than local grain was in small coastal ones. Saltwick ground its last sack of flour in 1921; its machinery was sold for scrap and the pond was drained for pasture. Many other mills met a similar fate."
          },
          {
            "label": "F",
            "text": "Today the surviving buildings attract a different kind of interest. Several have been restored as museums, and in one, volunteers fitted a small generator to the wheel so that it now produces enough electricity to light the exhibition rooms. Engineers who study the old sites point out that the original builders had already identified the key to a good location: a large difference between high and low water, combined with a narrow inlet that could be closed off cheaply."
          }
        ],
