        "id": "R2",
        "title": "Lichens: Partners on the Edge",
        "questionRange": "14-26",
        "paragraphs": [
          {
            "label": "A",
            "text": "A lichen is not a single organism but a partnership. It consists of a fungus living together with a photosynthesising partner, usually a microscopic alga or a type of bacterium known as a cyanobacterium. The fungus provides protection and gathers moisture and minerals, while its partner makes sugars from sunlight. When the Swiss botanist Simon Schwendener first proposed this dual nature in 1867, many of his colleagues rejected the idea, insisting that lichens were a distinct kind of plant. It took years of microscope work, and eventually laboratory experiments in which the partners were separated and recombined, to convince the scientific community that he was right."
          },
          {
            "label": "B",
            "text": "Few living things tolerate such harsh conditions. Lichens grow on bare rock in deserts, on the frozen ground of the Arctic tundra, and on the roofs of buildings in cities. Their secret lies in their ability to dry out almost completely without being damaged. A lichen in a dry period can lose most of its water and become dormant, resuming photosynthesis within minutes of the arrival of rain or fog. Because they do not depend on soil, they can colonise surfaces where no other organism could gain a foothold."
          },
          {
            "label": "C",
            "text": "Lichens are also among the slowest-growing organisms. Some crust-forming species expand by less than one millimetre a year, so a large patch on a boulder may be several hundred years old. Scientists have turned this slowness to advantage in a technique called lichenometry. By measuring the largest lichen on a rock surface, such as one exposed when a glacier retreated, they can estimate how long ago the surface became available for colonisation. The method has limits, however. It becomes unreliable for surfaces older than a few thousand years, because the rock eventually becomes so crowded that individual colonies can no longer be measured. Growth rates also vary with local climate, so researchers need nearby surfaces of known age to calibrate their results."
          },
          {
            "label": "D",
            "text": "A different kind of information comes from lichens' sensitivity to the air. Unlike plants, they have no roots or protective outer layer; they absorb water and dissolved substances directly from rain and the atmosphere. Consequently, pollutants build up in their tissues. During the industrial era, sulphur dioxide from burning coal killed lichens across many cities, leaving 'lichen deserts' on walls and trees. When clean-air laws reduced emissions, lichens gradually returned, and ecologists now survey which species grow on tree trunks to produce cheap maps of air quality."
          },
          {
            "label": "E",
            "text": "Lichens matter in other ways too. By releasing mild acids, they slowly break down rock surfaces, contributing to the first stages of soil formation. In northern regions, reindeer depend on certain lichens as a winter food when other vegetation lies buried under snow. Birds use them as nesting material, and people have extracted dyes from them for centuries; the litmus paper familiar from school chemistry laboratories was originally made from a lichen extract."
          },
          {
            "label": "F",
            "text": "The future of lichens is uncertain. Slow growth makes it hard for them to recolonise areas damaged by logging or development, and some mountain species may be unable to move uphill quickly enough as temperatures rise. Yet researchers are also drawn to their toughness. In laboratory experiments, certain lichens have survived conditions resembling those on Mars, which has led some scientists to ask whether similar organisms might help us understand where life could exist beyond Earth. Many basic questions remain unanswered, including how the partners first came together."
          }
        ],
