      {
        "id": "R3",
        "title": "Outsourcing the Mind",
        "questionRange": "27-40",
        "note": "The researchers and studies in this passage are fictional and were invented for this test.",
        "paragraphs": [
          {
            "label": "A",
            "text": "Few people today can recite more than a handful of telephone numbers. Directions, birthdays, appointments and facts that once had to be carried in the head are now stored in devices. Psychologists call this habit cognitive offloading: using physical actions or external tools to reduce the mental demands of a task. Writing a shopping list or tying a string around a finger are old examples of the same impulse. What has changed is not the habit itself but its scale and convenience, since a smartphone can now take over an astonishing range of mental chores."
          },
          {
            "label": "B",
            "text": "Early commentary was gloomy. In a series of experiments, the cognitive scientist Helena Voss asked volunteers to read forty short factual statements and type them into a computer. Half the volunteers were told that the computer would save what they typed; the others were told that it would be erased. In a later memory test, those who believed their statements had been saved recalled significantly fewer of them. Voss concluded that the brain adjusts how much effort it invests according to whether it expects to need the information again."
          },
          {
            "label": "C",
            "text": "The experiments had limitations, however. The volunteers were all university students, and the statements were trivial facts of no importance to them. Psychologist Idris Mbeki argued that offloading is not harmful in itself and may even be helpful. In his study, participants who were allowed to save routine information to a device went on to perform better on a demanding problem-solving task than participants who tried to keep everything in their heads. Mbeki suggested that offloading can release mental resources for more complex thinking, although he stressed that his results applied only to the kind of tasks he had tested."
          },
          {
            "label": "D",
            "text": "The benefits seem to depend on what is offloaded. Tomoko Arai, a researcher in spatial cognition, compared two groups of drivers over a period of one month. One group followed spoken turn-by-turn instructions; the other planned routes using paper maps. When both groups were later asked to find their way around the area unaided, those who had relied on instructions made more errors and drew less accurate sketch maps of the district. Arai proposed that the crucial difference lies in whether a tool replaces thinking or supports it: instructions replaced the driver's own mental map, whereas paper maps required the user to build one."
          },
          {
            "label": "E",
            "text": "There is also a question of confidence. Several studies indicate that people who search the internet frequently tend to overestimate their own knowledge, mistaking the ease of finding information for understanding it. The boundary between what we know and what we can look up appears to blur. This matters because a person who believes he or she understands a subject is unlikely to question it, or to check whether the information found is reliable."
          },
          {
            "label": "F",
            "text": "Educators have responded in different ways. Some schools have banned phones from classrooms; others teach what one headteacher calls 'offloading literacy', in which pupils learn to decide which information deserves memorising and which can safely be looked up. Supporters of this approach argue that memorised knowledge forms the base on which new information is judged and connected; without it, searching becomes aimless because the searcher does not know what to look for."
          },
          {
            "label": "G",
            "text": "The alarm that greets each new technology is rarely justified, and the same may be true here. Human memory has always been supported by tools, from writing to printing, and societies have adapted each time. Yet complacency is no wiser than panic. The real question is not whether we offload, but what we choose to offload, and whether we keep the ability to think when the device is switched off."
          }
        ],
